<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anaximenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anaximenes of Miletus was a Greek philosopher of the 6th century BCE, the third member of the Milesian school after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaximander">Anaximander</a>. He held that air was the underlying substance of all things, turning into fire when rarefied and into wind, water, earth and stone when condensed. His explanation of change through physical processes rather than myth made him an important figure in early Greek natural philosophy.</p></div>
